rformance-optimization","content":"Metadata Performance Optimization","locale":"en"},"3324":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-9","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#fuse2-optimization-solution","content":"FUSE2 Optimization Solution","locale":"en"},"3325":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-10","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","content":"This chapter introduces how to optimize Curvine's performance.","locale":"en"},"3326":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-11","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#server-side-performance-tuning","content":"The default configuration is suitable for most scenarios. In high-load environments, you can adjust the following parameters:","locale":"en"},"3327":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-12","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#server-side-performance-tuning","content":"**Thread count tuning**: Adjust the number of worker threads based on CPU cores and business load","locale":"en"},"3328":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-13","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#server-side-performance-tuning","content":"**Kernel parameter optimization**: Adjust network buffers, resource limits, and other parameters","locale":"en"},"3329":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-14","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#server-side-performance-tuning","content":"Curvine configuration adjustment example:","locale":"en"},"3330":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-15","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#server-side-performance-tuning","content":"Kernel parameter adjustment example (configuration examples show which parameters affect performance, for reference only, adjust according to actual needs):","locale":"en"},"3331":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-16","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#sequential-readwrite-scenarios","content":"Sequential read/write scenarios can achieve optimal performance using default configurations.","locale":"en"},"3332":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-17","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#high-concurrency-readwrite-scenarios","content":"When clients need to handle a large number of file I/O operations simultaneously:","locale":"en"},"3333":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-18","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#high-concurrency-readwrite-scenarios","content":"Set `read_chunk_num=1` and `write_chunk_num=1` to reduce memory usage","locale":"en"},"3334":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-19","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#high-concurrency-readwrite-scenarios","content":"Suitable for scenarios with concurrent file count > 1000","locale":"en"},"3335":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-20","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#high-concurrency-readwrite-scenarios","content":"Single file throughput will decrease, but overall throughput remains unchanged, memory usage will decrease by 90%","locale":"en"},"3336":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-21","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#high-concurrency-readwrite-scenarios","content":"Configuration example:","locale":"en"},"3337":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-22","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#large-file-sequential-read","content":"For sequential reading of single large files (>100GB):","locale":"en"},"3338":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-23","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#large-file-sequential-read","content":"Recommended parallelism = 4-16","locale":"en"},"3339":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-24","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#large-file-sequential-read","content":"Performance can improve 3-5 times, reaching 2-3GB/s","locale":"en"},"3340":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-25","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#large-file-sequential-read","content":"Configuration example:","locale":"en"},"3341":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-26","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#random-read","content":"For completely random reads, it's recommended to:","locale":"en"},"3342":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-27","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#random-read","content":"Set buffer to 1 to avoid read amplification","locale":"en"},"3343":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-28","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#random-read","content":"Make chunk_size close to average I/O size (e.g., 4KB/256KB)","locale":"en"},"3344":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-29","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#random-read","content":"For large files, set block_size ≥ 1GB to reduce connection count","locale":"en"},"3345":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-30","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#random-read","content":"Configuration example:","locale":"en"},"3346":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-31","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#random-read","content":"In completely random read scenarios, the client will cache connections with workers to reduce connection establishment overhead. However, if a file is very large, it will have many blocks, leading to too many connections. It's recommended to set block_size to 1GB or above when writing such large files to reduce connection count.","locale":"en"},"3347":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-32","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#fuse-performance-optimization","content":"Curvine FUSE currently supports FUSE2 and FUSE3. FUSE2's performance is approximately only 50% of FUSE3, so it's recommended to use FUSE3 in production environments.","locale":"en"},"3348":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-33","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#memory-control","content":"If FUSE is deployed on memory-constrained machines, you can set queue sizes to reduce memory usage:","locale":"en"},"3349":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-34","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#memory-control","content":"Configuration example:","locale":"en"},"3350":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-35","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#memory-control","content":"Curvine FUSE currently does not support page cache.","locale":"en"},"3351":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-36","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#metadata-performance-optimization","content":"In traditional libfuse implementations, metadata may be blocked by data read/write requests, causing request delays. Users are usually advised to set `attr_timeout` and `entry_timeout` to let the kernel cache metadata and improve performance.","locale":"en"},"3352":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-37","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#metadata-performance-optimization","content":"However, in Curvine's FUSE implementation, FUSE request processing is completely asynchronous, so there's no need to set `attr_timeout` and `entry_timeout`.","locale":"en"},"3353":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-38","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#metadata-performance-optimization","content":"Of course, if needed, you can also increase cache time. Configuration example:","locale":"en"},"3354":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-39","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#metadata-performance-optimization","content":"If you want modifications to be visible in real-time, you can set expiration time to 0, which won't affect FUSE performance:","locale":"en"},"3355":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-40","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#fuse2-optimization-solution","content":"If your environment can only use FUSE2 and you want to improve performance, you can use multiple mount points as a solution. In actual testing, 4-8 mount points can achieve about 80% of FUSE3's performance.","locale":"en"},"3356":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-41","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#fuse2-optimization-solution","content":"Configuration example:","locale":"en"},"3357":{"id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt-42","page_id":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/User-Manuals/opt#fuse2-optimization-solution","content":"Multiple mount points will mount multiple directories in the operating system. Programs need to use random or round-robin methods to select mount points to distribute the load.","locale":"en"},"3358":{"id":"/docs","page_id":"/docs","type":"page","content":"Curvine Documentation","tags":[],"url":"/docs","locale":"en"},"3359":{"id":"/zh-cn/docs/Architecture/fluid-integration","page_id":"/zh-cn/docs/Architecture/fluid-integration","type":"page","content":"Curvine 与 Fluid 集成","breadcrumbs":["Documents","Architecture"],"tags":[],"url":"/zh-cn/docs/Architecture/fluid-integration","locale":"zh-cn"},"3360":{"id":"/zh-cn/docs/Architecture/fluid-integration-0","page_id":"/zh-cn/docs/Architecture/fluid-integration","type":"h