 (QPS)"],[1858,"Curvine adopts a layered distributed architecture design that ensures clear component responsibilities with excellent scalability and high availability. The entire architecture is primarily divided into three layers: **control layer** (Master), **storage layer** (Workers), and **access layer** (clients: FUSE, SDK, CLI), which collaborate to complete data storage, processing, and management tasks."],[2025,"Curvine adopts a modular codebase. The main components in the current `main` branch are:"],[1909,"Curvine adopts distributed architecture design with multi-replica mechanisms and failover mechanisms to ensure system high availability:"],[2910,"Curvine also exposes a FUSE file system interface. After mounting it, for example via `bin/curvine-fuse.sh start`, you can operate on the mount point with normal Linux tools:"],[1,"Curvine and Fluid Integration"],[940,"Curvine commonly uses these inputs together:"],[1317,"Curvine config (`config.*`)"],[3329,"Curvine configuration adjustment example:"],[1859,"Curvine consists of three main server-side roles plus clients and UFS:"],[2276,"Curvine CSI has three moving parts:"],[5640,"Curvine CSI 主要由三部分组成："],[155,"Curvine design doc"],[3513,"Curvine design doc"],[3358,"Curvine Documentation"],[2590,"Curvine does not follow Close-to-Open. Instead, it is fully consistent with local disk file semantics:"],[1922,"Curvine exposes multiple access paths for analytics, AI, and data infrastructure workloads, including the Rust CLI, FUSE, and Java/Python SDK bindings."],[2509,"Curvine follows local disk usage habits."],[3350,"Curvine FUSE currently does not support page cache."],[3347,"Curvine FUSE currently supports FUSE2 and FUSE3. FUSE2's performance is approximately only 50% of FUSE3, so it's recommended to use FUSE3 in production environments."],[3250,"Curvine FUSE is fully asynchronous — `attr_timeout` and `entry_timeout` are optional. Increase them for read-heavy workloads with stable metadata:"],[6612,"Curvine FUSE 